Hello All,

There is an informal rule of thumb that one step of DR is acceptable at a f=
usion joint.

There is no official documentation of this as a recommended practice in any=
 industry standard or at the PPI however.  It is all word of mouth.  The un=
derstanding is that it would be at the user=92s risk.

The general availability of fittings with machined ends (where the ends are=
 machined to match the pipe) is for the purpose of matching at the joint wi=
th the pipe.  This can be done with fabricated and molded fittings.  We hav=
e the capability to do this and have done it.  Pricing for fabricated fitti=
ngs are in the price sheets.  Fittings with machined ends will cost more.

The fusion parameters would have to be adjusted  for mismatching DR=92s but=
 there is no official industry guideline for this either.  Again this is at=
 user=92s risk.

When the mismatch gets too great there are two problems that occur.  The fu=
sion joint quality is questionable and the creation of a geometric stress r=
iser (the step itself).  This is mostly an issue at fitting to pipe connect=
ions.  The fusion joint quality is questionable because the ideal fusion pa=
rameters are more different for the larger mismatch.

Brandon Wheeler of Nupi has long experience with these matters working at J=
M Eagles fitting plant.  He says he has seen ample evidence from failures t=
hat two DR steps is too much.

Defining a DR step has some peculiarities because there are =93half step=94=
 DR=92s

From thicker to thinner the full step DR=92s for IPS pipe go like this:
7
9
11
13.5
17
21
26
32.5

DR 15.5 is a =93half step=94 DR.  The rule of thumb would say a DR11 fittin=
g should have ends machined to DR 15.5 or at least to DR 13.5 when used wit=
h DR 15.5 pipe.

Our thoughts on this if asked:

Harco advises that good practice is to match the ends at a joint.  There is=
 an unofficial rule of thumb that a mismatch of one DR step can be acceptab=
le but to do so would be the users decision and risk.  Our observation to d=
ate in the golf application is that one step in DR across a joint seems not=
 to have resulted in problems to this date.  We must caution that the life =
of PE systems in golf applications is very short and that the problems may =
not manifest themselves for some time.  It is only in the last 4 years that=
 pipe thinner than DR 11 has been in regular use.  These systems would use =
DR13.5 pipe and DR11 fittings.

John

